{
  "result": "On the first warm Saturday in March, someone left a lemon on the windowsill of the laundromat. It was not in a bag or beside a forgotten basket. It sat alone in a square of sun, with a small green leaf still attached.\n\nBy noon, everyone had an opinion. The owner assumed it belonged to the woman who cleaned the dryers with vinegar. A boy in soccer cleats said it was a prize and asked what he had to do to win it. His father, folding a fitted sheet with mounting resentment, told him to ask the lemon.\n\nI was there because my washing machine had stopped draining. I had brought a book, but the lemon was easier to pay attention to. It demanded nothing. Even the book wanted me to remember which brother had sold the horse.\n\nNear closing, an older woman came in without any laundry. She went straight to the window and picked up the lemon. Then she took another from her coat pocket and set it in the same spot.\n\n\u201cIs that yours?\u201d I asked.\n\n\u201cThis one is,\u201d she said, holding up the first lemon. \u201cI left it here this morning to see if anybody would take it.\u201d\n\n\u201cWhy leave another?\u201d\n\n\u201cWell,\u201d she said, looking at the windowsill, \u201cI brought it in case somebody had.\u201d\n\nThe next Saturday, my machine was working again. I walked past the laundromat anyway. There was an orange on the sill. Someone had drawn a face on it in blue pen.",
